Z||000000||blkcofy||07-10-2001||09:19 PM||||I posted a similar message on one of the older topics, but I figured I'd get a bigger response by posting on the main forum.<P>I typically don't engage in conversations on the internet, but I've never found a forum that made my heart skip a beat like this one.  (Okay, at least one that didn't require me to wait for my wife to fall asleep!...just joking)  Anyway, I just discovered this site, and I've been grinning ever since.  I have recently turned my focus away from my daily driver and back where it belongs...My White 1987 Fiero GT.  <P>My first of many questions from the new guy. My fiero has sat undisturbed and garaged for the last 24 months. And even then, I haven't driven it daily since 1995.  It's been garaged, so visually, it's still in great condition.  I've got about 73M miles on it, and the only major repair has been a new clutch system and complete brake lines and pads.  I finally got it started last week, after having to install a new battery and battery connectors from The Fiero Store (free plug!)  I've driven it around a bit, and was even brave enough to pull it to 80mph.  To my excitement, it didn't even hesitate, and still stayed well below 3000rms in 5th gear.  It does idle very rough, so I assume I need to get a serious tune up since it's sat for so long. In my younger days, I replace the stock exhaust for a straight pipe (w/out muffler).  It's clearly too loud for me now, so I'm in need of a good performance exhaust system, and was looking at The Fiero Store at the Ocelot system. Any advice on exhaust systems? <P>The bigger question is what are the main things (a checklist if you will) that I should be checking, to make sure everything is working properly before I get too excited about this REBIRTH. Should I have all the liquids drained and replaced? I've got a wish list a arm long of how I want to upgrade my fiero, but I think the first thing is to get it running smoothly, before I start adding stuff. What do you guys/gals think? My other car is a Contour SVT (which I love) but I can see one day selling that car and it makes little sense to put alot more money into it in aftermarket stuff. I've already spent a good $6,000 in tires, audio, aesthetics, exhaust, ect. I was about to look into a supercharger, until I saw the "FAST & FURIOUS" movie.  If I'm going to make that kind of investment, and hopefully drive another "neck turner", I'd be better off getting my fiero back on the road...right?  I don't think I'd EVER part with it, so why not? Since finding this forum, I now have a sense of all the possibilities for this car. Any advice you folks can give me would be greatly appreciated.<P><BR>||||reg||10
Z||000001||skitime||07-10-2001||09:25 PM||||Welcome to the forum. You have found a huge resource for you Fiero.  I am sure someone in the forum is from your area and could reccomend a garage or good mechanic to tune it.  The white GTs are really sharp.<P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL><BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pictures-1_5.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||07-10-2001||09:48 PM||||I caan see replacing the fluids in the car.  A tune up.  The distributor cap.  Check the corrosion on the distributor.  Maybe the cause of the rough idle.  Check for error codes. Couls be some of the sensors have pooped out. I replaced the exhaust on my 87 with a Borla system.  5th gear for me at 70 is 2400rpm.<P>Make sure all the brake components are working along with the E brake.<P>You don't see too many Fieros on the road, so it may be a neck turner.  Not as quick as the "Fast and Furious" rice cars.  But a fun car to drive.||||reg||1
